  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1238 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nAdditional rain chances move back into the CWA Friday night and\nSaturday with the parent mid level shortwave progged to push\neast/northeast over the region. At this time, the greater rain\nchances are forecast for southeast Oklahoma into western Arkansas\nthrough the day Saturday, and then over northwest Arkansas into\nSaturday night before the shortwave departs. Timing wise, rain\nchances begin to taper off Saturday afternoon/evening from west to\neast and exit early Sunday evening. Thunder probs with the\nshortwave are slightly higher, over southeast Oklahoma into west\ncentral Arkansas with the base of the shortwave. For now will\ncarry slight chance of thunder for far southeast Oklahoma into \nwest central Arkansas Saturday afternoon/evening. Rainfall amounts\nthrough Saturday night are forecast to be generally less than a \nhalf inch. \n\nCloud cover holds on over the region Sunday in the wake of the \ndeparting shortwave. Also Sunday/Sunday night, mid/upper heights\nbegin to rise with an increase in southerly low level flow. In\nresponse, Sunday afternoon temperatures in the 70s are forecast, \nwhile temperatures remain warm with the breezy southerly winds \nSunday night. These winds continue to increase during the day \nMonday ahead of a stronger low pressure system and associated cold\nfront approaching from the west. \n\nAn area of low pressure off the Pacific northwest coast is progged\nto drop east-southeast over the weekend and merge with a secondary\nlow moving into the CONUS within the subtropical jet. The new\nparent low pressure system approaches the CWA Monday and moves\ninto the region Monday night and Tuesday. Shower and also\nthunderstorm chances begin to increase Monday, and become more\nlikely Monday night/Tuesday as the cold front makes its way\nthrough the CWA. Increasing instability as well as a greater plume\nof moisture spreading over the CWA ahead of the front will aid in\nat least limited potential for strong/severe storms. Also, shear\nvectors look to align along the front as it moves through which\nwill also aid in a heavy rain threat. Details will continue to\nevolve as we approach the event, though the potential for impacts\nhas increased. \n\nThe low pressure system looks to exit during the middle of next\nweek, though differences remain with the potential for another\ndisturbance to drop into the region on the backside of the \nexiting mid/upper level trof axis. However, one thing to note is \nbehind the cold front, more seasonal temperatures for early \nNovember could move into the region for the second half of next\nweek.",
